WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:05.480
All right, moving on to the next step.

00:05.480 --> 00:09.360
Now we're going to work with multiple queries inside one stored procedure.

00:09.360 --> 00:11.040
And this is what we have learned at the start.

00:11.040 --> 00:15.200
We can have multiple SQL statements in one stored procedure.

00:15.200 --> 00:17.840
And now we have a new report and query to generate.

00:17.880 --> 00:21.720
It says find the total number of orders and the total sales.

00:21.720 --> 00:22.640
So let's do it quickly.

00:22.640 --> 00:23.920
We can write it like this.

00:24.160 --> 00:29.960
Select counts order ID this is the total orders.

00:30.480 --> 00:37.640
And then the sum of sales total sales from our table sales orders.

00:37.800 --> 00:41.600
And of course we are always creating a report based on specific country.

00:41.600 --> 00:46.880
So that means we have to go and join it with the customers table in order to filter the data.

00:47.160 --> 00:51.440
So on customer ID equal to the customer ID.

00:52.120 --> 00:53.960
And now we're going to go and filter the data.

00:54.280 --> 00:57.560
So country equal to USA.

00:58.040 --> 00:59.320
So something like this.

00:59.320 --> 01:01.000
Let's go and execute it.

01:01.040 --> 01:02.850
And with that for the US customers.

01:02.850 --> 01:06.010
We have six orders and the total sales 180.

01:06.050 --> 01:08.450
And of course, the same thing we're going to do for Germany.

01:08.450 --> 01:11.970
So now, of course, we will not go and create an extra stored procedure for this.

01:11.970 --> 01:14.970
We're going to go and put everything in one stored procedure.

01:14.970 --> 01:18.650
So let's go and copy the whole thing and put it here inside.

01:19.330 --> 01:22.610
So after the first report we're going to have the second report.

01:22.610 --> 01:23.930
And now the best practice here.

01:23.930 --> 01:30.090
If you have multiple queries in stored procedure go and add at the end of each query a semicolon.

01:30.090 --> 01:31.930
It is just easier to understand how.

01:31.970 --> 01:36.770
Now this is the end of this query, especially if you have like a big complex queries where you have

01:36.810 --> 01:38.730
CTE, union and so on.

01:38.770 --> 01:43.970
It's going to be really hard to understand that we are talking now about completely new query, but

01:43.970 --> 01:48.770
it is not like something that database requires it, but it's just easier to read.

01:48.770 --> 01:51.490
So just add semicolons at the end of each query.

01:51.490 --> 01:56.450
So now let's go and execute the whole thing in order to change the definition of our query.

01:56.450 --> 02:00.450
And one more thing of course, don't forget we don't need static values over here.

02:00.450 --> 02:03.170
We're going to go and add our nice parameters.

02:03.940 --> 02:06.020
So add country.

02:06.020 --> 02:09.940
So I think with that we have everything is ready to be executed.

02:09.940 --> 02:12.820
So let's go and change the definition of our stored procedure.

02:12.820 --> 02:17.260
And now let's go and start with the defaults where the country equal to USA.

02:17.300 --> 02:18.740
So let's go and execute it.

02:18.740 --> 02:21.340
And now in the output as you can see we have two results.

02:21.340 --> 02:23.460
And that's because we have two queries.

02:23.460 --> 02:28.900
So the first report is for the first query and the second one for the new one that we just created.

02:28.900 --> 02:34.140
And the same thing if you go and execute the stored procedure for Germany we will get as well two results.

02:34.140 --> 02:38.500
And here we can see we have four orders and 200 of total sales for Germany.

02:38.500 --> 02:40.140
So as you can see it's very simple.

02:40.140 --> 02:44.060
You can go now and add multiple SQL statements, not only queries.

02:44.060 --> 02:45.620
You can go and update.

02:45.620 --> 02:49.180
You can do an insert, delete any kind of SQL statements.

02:49.180 --> 02:52.340
You can just go and add it inside your program.

02:52.340 --> 02:55.860
And as usual, SQL can execute it from the top to the bottom.

02:55.860 --> 02:59.580
So since this is the first SQL statement, it's going to execute it first.

02:59.860 --> 03:01.980
And then after that it's going to go to the next one.

03:01.980 --> 03:06.340
So this is how you can add multiple SQL statements to your stored procedure.
